# Go Style Guide

Follow [Effective Go](https://go.dev/doc/effective_go), [Go Code Review Comments](https://go.dev/wiki/CodeReviewComments), and the [Google Go Style Guide](https://google.github.io/styleguide/go/). This rule records the conventions that matter most here and resolves choices where idioms vary.

- Layering, DDD entities, services, and handlers: see `architecture.mdc`
- Tests: see `go-testing-practices.mdc`

## Formatting and Naming

- Code is `gofmt` / `goimports` clean; never hand-align or argue formatting
- **MixedCaps** everywhere; no underscores in Go identifiers (test function names excepted)
- **Initialisms keep their case**: `userID`, `HTTPClient`, `ServeHTTP`, `parseURL` (not `userId`, `HttpClient`)
- **Packages**: short, lowercase, single word (`auth`, `domain`, `service`); avoid `util`, `common`, `helpers`, `base`
- **No stutter**: the package name is part of the identifier (`user.Service`, `http.Client`; not `user.UserService`)
- **Getters drop `Get`**: `u.Email()`, not `u.GetEmail()`; setters use `SetEmail`
- **Interfaces**: single-method interfaces are named method + `-er` (`Reader`, `Validator`, `Notifier`); larger interfaces get a descriptive noun (`UserRepository`)
- **Receivers**: one or two letters reflecting the type (`u *User`, `s *UserService`), consistent across all methods; never `this` or `self`
- **Receiver kind**: if any method needs a pointer receiver, use pointer receivers for all methods of that type
- **Variable names**: short for short scopes (`i`, `r`, `buf`), more descriptive the farther they are used from their declaration

## Control Flow

Handle the error or edge case first and return early; keep the happy path at minimal indentation.

```go
// ✅ GOOD: Early return, happy path unindented
user, err := s.repo.GetByID(ctx, id)
if err != nil {
    return nil, fmt.Errorf("get user %s: %w", id, err)
}
return user, nil

// ❌ BAD: else after return, nested happy path
user, err := s.repo.GetByID(ctx, id)
if err == nil {
    return user, nil
} else {
    return nil, err
}
```

## Errors

- Return errors explicitly; never discard them with `_` unless a comment explains why it is safe
- Wrap with context using `%w`; the message is lowercase, has no trailing punctuation, and does not start with "failed to" or "error"
- Compare with `errors.Is` / `errors.As`; never compare `err.Error()` strings
- Either handle an error (log, retry, degrade) or return it, not both
- Sentinel errors are package-level `var ErrXxx = errors.New("...")`; custom error types end in `Error`
- No `panic` in request paths; reserve it for programmer errors at startup

```go
// ✅ GOOD: Short lowercase context, wrapped with %w
if err != nil {
    return fmt.Errorf("get user %s: %w", id, err)
}

// ❌ BAD: Redundant prefix, capitalized, loses the error chain
if err != nil {
    return fmt.Errorf("Failed to get user: %v", err)
}

// ❌ BAD: Logs and returns, so the same error is reported twice
if err != nil {
    log.Printf("get user: %v", err)
    return err
}
```

## Interfaces and Types

- Define interfaces in the **consuming** package, not next to the implementation
- **Accept interfaces, return concrete types**
- Keep interfaces small; don't add an interface until there is a second implementation or a test seam needs it
- Make the zero value useful where practical (`var buf bytes.Buffer`, `var mu sync.Mutex`)
- Use keyed fields in composite literals for types from other packages
- Don't copy values that contain a `sync.Mutex` or `sync.WaitGroup`; use pointer receivers on such types

```go
// ✅ GOOD: Keyed fields
cfg := &config.Server{
    Addr:         ":8080",
    ReadTimeout:  5 * time.Second,
    WriteTimeout: 10 * time.Second,
}

// ❌ BAD: Positional fields break when the struct changes
cfg := &config.Server{":8080", 5 * time.Second, 10 * time.Second}
```

## Context and Concurrency

- `ctx context.Context` is the first parameter of any function that does I/O or may block; name it `ctx`
- Never store a `context.Context` in a struct; pass it explicitly
- Use `context.Background()` only in `main`, initialization, and tests; propagate the incoming `ctx` everywhere else
- Every goroutine has a clear owner and exit condition (ctx cancellation, closed channel, `errgroup`); no fire-and-forget goroutines
- Prefer `errgroup.WithContext` for parallel work that can fail
- Use `sync.Mutex` to guard state; use channels to transfer ownership or signal
- Use directional channel types in signatures (`<-chan T`, `chan<- T`)

```go
// ✅ GOOD: Goroutines bound to ctx, errors collected
g, ctx := errgroup.WithContext(ctx)
for _, id := range ids {
    g.Go(func() error {
        return s.sync(ctx, id)
    })
}
if err := g.Wait(); err != nil {
    return fmt.Errorf("sync users: %w", err)
}
```

## Functions and Declarations

- Avoid mutable package-level state and `init()` side effects; wire dependencies through constructors
- Constructors are `NewXxx`; return `(T, error)` when construction can fail
- Use named results only when they document meaning (e.g. two values of the same type); avoid naked returns outside very short functions
- Use `defer` for cleanup (`Close`, `Unlock`, `Rollback`) right after acquiring the resource
- Prefer `any` over `interface{}`
- Use generics only when they remove real duplication; don't add type parameters speculatively
- Return a `nil` slice for "no results"; use an empty slice only when the distinction matters (e.g. JSON `[]` vs `null`)
- Prefer the standard library; add third-party packages only when stdlib is insufficient

## Doc Comments

- Every exported identifier has a doc comment
- Comments are full sentences that begin with the identifier's name and end with a period
- Describe behavior and contracts in prose; don't list parameters and return values
- Package comments start with `// Package xxx ...` in exactly one file

```go
// ✅ GOOD
// GetUser returns the user with the given ID, or ErrNotFound if none exists.
func (s *UserService) GetUser(ctx context.Context, id string) (*domain.User, error)

// ❌ BAD: Not a sentence, Javadoc-style parameter list
// get user
// @param id the user id
// @return user, error
func (s *UserService) GetUser(ctx context.Context, id string) (*domain.User, error)
```

## Performance

- Profile (`pprof`, benchmarks) before optimizing
- Preallocate slices and maps when the size is known (`make([]T, 0, n)`)
- Use `strings.Builder` for building strings in loops
